usage: benchmark_conversion_memory.py [-h] [--sizes-mib SIZES_MIB]
                                      [--input-kind {module,pt2}]
                                      [--identity-mul] [--modes MODES]
                                      [--tables TABLES] [--width WIDTH]
                                      [--seed SEED] [--threads THREADS]
                                      [--optimization {O0,O1}]
                                      [--repeat REPEAT]
                                      [--flatbuffer-limit FLATBUFFER_LIMIT]
                                      [--baseline-root BASELINE_ROOT]
                                      [--workdir WORKDIR] [--keep-outputs]
                                      [--json]

Compare complete bytes conversion with explicit file conversion on CPU. Each
measurement runs in a fresh, sequential subprocess. Module measurements
include touched source weights and export; PT2 measurements include loading a
fixture exported in an unmeasured subprocess. Elapsed time covers the
conversion API and save only. Streaming file hashing, structural verification,
constant/metadata equivalence, and reference numerical checks run outside that
measurement. The driver never loads a complete model binary. Synthetic models
sum embedding lookups from deterministic FP32 tables. Every constant survives
conversion, while activations and numerical probes stay small as weight size
increases. These are serialization-oriented full-conversion measurements, not
a prediction for every model architecture or target backend.

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --sizes-mib SIZES_MIB
  --input-kind {module,pt2}
  --identity-mul
  --modes MODES
  --tables TABLES
  --width WIDTH
  --seed SEED
  --threads THREADS
  --optimization {O0,O1}
  --repeat REPEAT
  --flatbuffer-limit FLATBUFFER_LIMIT
  --baseline-root BASELINE_ROOT
  --workdir WORKDIR
  --keep-outputs
  --json                Print JSON only to stdout.
